 \cortext[cor1]{Corresponding author: Yunfei Yin (yunfeiyin@hit.edu.cn).}
 
 \begin{abstract}
-Transport scenario analysis requires fast traveler models that reproduce how choices change, not only which mode is most likely. We develop a behavioral distillation framework that learns mode probabilities and departure adjustments from an offline large language model (LLM), compares the resulting responses with stated choices, and converts them into executable demand. Controlled experiments with four neural objectives and three training seeds show that paired supervision reduces probability-response error by 4.97\% relative to pointwise distribution matching. A linear choice model achieves still lower response error. Combining it with an independent neural departure predictor gives a compact, 18,733-parameter model with a departure mean absolute error of 7.61 minutes against the Teacher, within the range of jointly trained neural models. Comparisons with 332 Singapore and 321 Shanghai respondents reveal different strengths across delays, weather and accessibility changes. Additional Teacher predictions for 24 respondents per city help locate these differences: the Singapore accessibility response is attenuated in both the Teacher and a supply-adapted Student, whereas that Student substantially exaggerates the encoded Shanghai delay response. In a 1,000-person Helsinki experiment, route-feasibility screening removes public-transport fallback but does not necessarily bring executed responses closer to predicted ones. The results support modular traveler models for efficient scenario construction and identify where behavioral comparison and transport execution influence their use.
+Transport scenario analysis requires traveler models that preserve changes in choice, not only baseline predictions. We study behavioral distillation from a large language model (LLM) by separating fidelity to Teacher responses, agreement with stated behavior, and preservation of predicted responses in simulation. In a controlled benchmark with six held-out synthetic personas and three training seeds, paired supervision reduces probability-response error by 4.97\% relative to pointwise distribution matching. A linear choice Student achieves lower response error than the tested neural models. Combining it with an independent neural departure predictor yields a mean absolute error of 7.61 minutes against the Teacher, within the range of the joint-model means. Comparisons with 332 Singapore and 321 Shanghai respondents show intervention-specific agreement under the questionnaire-to-model specifications. Contemporary Teacher predictions for 24 respondents per city separate Teacher--human discrepancies from Student--Teacher differences: Singapore's accessibility response is attenuated in both the Teacher and a supply-adapted Student, whereas that Student exaggerates Shanghai's encoded delay response. In a fixed-timetable experiment with 1,000 Helsinki travelers, feasibility-constrained assignment improves routability but does not necessarily reduce the predicted-to-simulated response gap. The findings support separate choice and timing specifications and evaluation against the behavioral outcome and intervention of interest.
 \end{abstract}
 
