 \label{sec:intro}
 Transport scenario analysis asks how travelers will respond when the conditions of a trip change. A public-transport delay may shift some travelers to driving, leave others on the same service, and cause others to adjust their departure time. Rain, higher fares and longer walks to a stop create different responses, even among people making similar trips. Agent-based simulation represents the interaction of these decisions with transport supply \citep{horni2016multi,axhausen2002some}. Its usefulness therefore depends on the changes in demand that a behavioral model produces, as well as its baseline mode shares.
 
-Large language models (LLMs) offer a flexible way to describe such decisions. A prompt can combine a traveler's circumstances, available modes and trip attributes with a contextual change. Recent transportation studies use this capability for travel-behavior prediction, route-choice agents and agent-based modeling \citep{mo2026llmtravel,wang2025agentic,liu2025framework,nie2025roles}. Direct use at population scale, however, requires repeated remote calls. Behavioral distillation offers a different arrangement: the LLM supplies supervision offline, and a compact numerical model makes the repeated decisions needed for simulation.
+Large language models (LLMs) offer a flexible way to describe such decisions. A prompt can combine a traveler's circumstances, available modes and trip attributes with a contextual change. Recent transportation studies use this capability for travel-behavior prediction, route-choice agents and agent-based modeling \citep{mo2026llmtravel,wang2025agentic,liu2025framework,nie2025roles}. Direct use at population scale, however, requires repeated LLM evaluation. Behavioral distillation provides an alternative: the LLM supplies supervision offline, and a compact numerical model makes the repeated decisions needed for simulation.
 
 The central question is what that compact model should preserve. Reproducing the Teacher's most likely mode at a baseline state does not ensure that the model responds similarly to a delay or fare change. Even two models with the same leading choice can redistribute probability very differently. We call agreement at individual states \emph{static fidelity}, and agreement in the change between paired states \emph{response fidelity}. For scenario analysis, the latter determines how a modeled intervention changes demand.
 
